STAGED PATCH 0001 (kernel round) of 5: apply FIRST (the
doors call this core).
Targets: Linux 6.12.116. New directory mm/iscandar/,
new header include/linux/iscandar.h, one line in
mm/Makefile, one placement block in
arch/x86/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S. Context lines are
schematic where they touch existing files; the new
files are staged in full. Re-base on the real tree
before applying.

Subject: iscandar: the in-kernel core (mass, engine,
atomic glue, canary, registry, state read)

This patch replaces the bridge core (bridge round
0005) wholesale. What the bridge core owned across a
channel, this core owns in place: the engine itself,
seated over the marked mass; the arena as a section of
the kernel image; the registry in the mass's third
region; the prefix machinery; and the state read. The
channel, the records, the rings, the raise, and the
boot parameters for channel and arena addresses do not
exist here (design sections 1 and 2: the span identity
check retires with the channel; there is only the
linker's placement to verify).

Copied verbatim at application time, never retyped
(the bridge set's rule, kept):
- mm/iscandar/iscandar_mass.s, from
  build/kernel/iscandar_mass.s (generated by
  build/kernel/gen_mass.py from the constants of
  record; ../MASS-LAYOUT.md is its layout document).
- The engine sources, from their homes of record
  (build/Makefile's ENGINE_SRCS and GEARS lists):
  c-translation/k4 (Beam, BeamCoordinate, CPUPointer,
  CubeEngine), c-translation/k3 (Engine, Modes, Mode,
  Nano, Tiny, Small, Large, Huge, Cube, Plane,
  Coordinate, Bijection, MPUPointer, Nothing, Error),
  and the gear bodies from the engine repository
  (Mark, Unmark, Commit, RowsCheck, PlanesCheck,
  PairsRowDriver, PlaneSmallDriver), with their
  headers. R9: one engine text, byte-for-byte the
  harness's.
- include/iscandar_bridge.h, from
  build/bridge/iscandar_bridge.h, as in the bridge
  round: it remains the constants' one home. This
  build uses its constants and center numbers only;
  its channel and record definitions are dead here
  and are not referenced.

The glue's shape, against the design:

- Grants (R2, R3, section 3). iscandar_alloc takes a
  byte size, serves whole pages at the RAM build's
  standing calibration (one engine sector to one
  page), and returns an arena pointer or NULL. The
  engine call runs inside the core's one
  spin_lock_irqsave section; nothing inside it
  sleeps, and the sleep-free audit is a build gate on
  these objects (verification step 2). Every grant is
  page-aligned by construction (whole pages from a
  page-aligned section), so any alignment up to
  PAGE_SIZE is honored by construction; the doors
  that can express a larger alignment refuse it in
  their own rejection sets, as the percpu door does.
  A request above one cube (32,768 pages) cannot be
  served, a fit never crosses a cube wall: the glue
  refuses it, counted, NULL. A zero-size request
  returns ZERO_SIZE_PTR, stock's own shape, and the
  free path ignores it by the range test.
- The extent ledger (the layer's bookkeeping, moved
  in unchanged). At grant, the grant's first page bin
  takes the page count; it must read zero before, and
  a nonzero bin under a fresh grant means the books
  disagree, which is an invariant violation: the
  grant is returned to the engine, the violation is
  counted and printed, NULL is the answer. At free,
  the bin gives the count; a bin reading zero is a
  free of what was never granted (or a second free),
  counted and printed, never crashed on (R12). One
  difference from the layer, deliberate: the layer
  carried the fixture count in bin 0 of each cube as
  a sentinel; the mass's ledger is born zero
  (../MASS-LAYOUT.md, desk check 1), so the kernel
  ledger has no sentinel and needs none, since
  fixture pages are marked in the store and are
  never granted.
- The registry (section 1: bookkeeping beside the
  doors). Entries live in the mass's third region at
  the fixed 64-byte stride. Registration is a control
  path act: process context, the control mutex,
  confirm-then-commit (the entry is staged locally
  and published by writing the cache pointer last).
  Registration does not wait for ready: the registry
  is plain mass memory and a static mutex, so a cache
  born in the prefix registers at birth, and the
  bridge's raise-time registration pass has no
  counterpart because there is no raise. The cap is
  the verdict, as the bridge staging ruled: a full
  registry fails the creation, loudly.
- The gen_pool gate (the audit's item 3, the backing
  rule). The decision is recorded per pool where its
  chunks are added (0005 hooks gen_pool_add_owner and
  gen_pool_add_virt): a chunk is served only if its
  whole physical range is ordinary system RAM, tested
  page by page with the tree's own RAM test. A pool
  is served while every chunk added so far has
  passed; a failing chunk marks the pool stock
  permanently, and live grants at that moment are a
  finding, printed. The doors consult the recorded
  decision; the state read lists pools and decisions
  (the T6 comparison reads it).
- The prefix (R11). Until ready opens, iscandar_
  active() is false and every door passes stock,
  counted per center and logged in the core's ring.
  The prefix ends at this core's init, not at a
  userland raise: postcore_initcall, late enough that
  the mass and the proc filesystem stand, early
  enough that all userland traffic is served. Boot
  traffic before it is the prefix: named, bounded,
  counted, exactly as R11 states.
- The canary (R7, section 5). At init, before ready:
  the mass's consistency is verified from its own
  bytes (per cube, the store's popcount, the row sum,
  the plane sum, count_overall, and its mirror must
  all agree; the ledger and the registry must read
  zero), and the witness word (cube 0, store word 0,
  the fixture word 0x07FFFFFF of ../MASS-LAYOUT.md)
  is read by three paths: by byte offset from the
  mass base, through the Beam struct, and through
  the page path (virt_to_page and back). Agreement
  opens ready. Any disagreement keeps the engine
  shut for the boot: state closed, stock serves, and
  the refusal prints the addresses that disagreed.
  Init verifies; it does not fill (R8).
- The hold. iscandar_hold on the command line leaves
  the engine shut for the boot, state closed, stock
  serving: the degrade proof's instrument
  (verification step 6), an ordinary boot flag, not
  an error path.
- The latency ledger (section 5). The glue samples
  get_cycles() around the engine call, inside the
  section, and keeps the worst per class and a count
  per class, classes by request size in pages at the
  engine's own gear boundaries (Nano to 8, Tiny to
  32, Small to 64, Large to 1024, Huge to 32767,
  CubeCheck at 32768), frees classed by the freed
  run's length. The walk itself is untouched;
  witnesses stand at the doors. Divided into
  known-step calls at the bench, this closes R5.
- The state read, /proc/iscandar. A window, not a
  source (section 1): its first line is the state
  line in the three-way form, exactly "state
  prefix", "state ready", or "state closed". Then
  the books, the counters (prefix per center, the
  watch counter, refusals, violations, live pages),
  the latency worsts, the registry's occupancy, the
  gen_pool decisions, and the prefix log. It is
  created before the canary runs, so a closed boot
  still reports itself closed, with the refusal's
  figures. There is no write side; the raise verb
  died with the bridge.

